- DONE (2026-08-28) Deleted `sitecustomize.py` (dead since the day it was added).
  It was introduced by 0d00980 "final attempt to fix Qwen before switching models" -- and the
  models were switched; Qwen is not in eval_config.json. CPython auto-imports `sitecustomize`
  only from a directory on `PYTHONPATH`, never from the script's own directory (verified in
  both the old flat layout and the new one), and every SLURM script does
  `unset PYTHONHOME PYTHONPATH`, so it never once executed. Its contents were superseded by
  `src/train_unsloth.py:5-10`, which sets the same six env vars unconditionally before torch
  is imported -- broader coverage than sitecustomize's `OCPQ_STABILIZE_RUNTIME`-gated version.
  The only thing lost is the Python-level `tqdm.tqdm.monitor_interval = 0` (tqdm does NOT read
  TQDM_MONITOR_INTERVAL from the environment -- the class attribute stays 10). That is provably
  not load-bearing: the monitor thread has been alive in every training run to date and all
  four adapters trained fine. If a tqdm monitor-thread hang ever reappears, set it explicitly
  in `src/train_unsloth.py` rather than resurrecting a magic module.
  The dead `OCPQ_STABILIZE_RUNTIME` export in `slurm/submit_job_qwen3_5_4b.sh:99,117` is left
  in place: that whole script is superseded, and it also exports the six vars directly anyway.

- PARKED (2026-08-29) Directions considered after the full matrix came back at 2.31% exact-match
  on the discriminating stratum and the supervisor's verdict that "with those numbers you cannot
  claim anything with regards to it being of sufficient quality to solve the problem that you
  motivate". Self-consistency is being done first (see the separate entry below); these four are
  parked with their reasoning so the analysis behind them is not lost.

  AGENTIC WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS (3-5 weeks). The single largest untapped signal. The model
  currently receives a type-level schema (`build_schema_block`, src/LLM.py). CORRECTED 2026-09-02,
  this entry overstated what is withheld: at level `full` the prompt DOES carry object/event
  attributes with bounded string-value enumerations (`(String: v1, v2)`) and the E2O/O2O graphs
  WITH qualifiers, plus an instruction to use only those relations. What it never carries is
  anything COUNTED or INSTANCE-level -- how many events of each type, activity frequencies,
  relation cardinality (the graph says `orders -> items` exists, not that an order has ~3 items),
  numeric attribute ranges, the log's timespan -- or what its own query returns before committing.
  That is exactly where the 1354
  `wrong_population` failures live: a plausible-looking entity/relation combination that does not
  hold in the log. Tool surface: `count(type)`, `sample(type, n)`,
  `relation_density(event_type, object_type)`, `attribute_values(type, attr)`, and
  `try_query(partial)` returning size plus sample bindings without committing. Then plan -> probe
  -> draft -> execute -> check cardinality against expectation -> revise.
  Caveat that makes it a different study: 3B-14B open models are weak at multi-step tool use, so
  this realistically needs a frontier API model or a strong tool-calling local model. That changes
  the premise from "can a fine-tuned small open model do this" -- but it supplies the thing the
  paper currently lacks, which is an UPPER BOUND. Right now there is a floor and no ceiling, which
  is precisely why the "cannot claim anything" critique lands. If an agentic frontier model reaches
  ~50-60%, the task is solvable and "small fine-tuned models cannot yet" becomes a bounded, honest
  finding; if it reaches ~15%, the bottleneck is the formalism or the benchmark rather than the
  models, which is a more interesting result and one nobody has published.

  EXPLICIT SCHEMA LINKING (1-2 weeks) -- RETIRED 2026-08-30, do not build this. Two stages instead
  of one: first ask which event and object types the question involves, then generate constrained to
  that selection. Attacks hallucinated entities (14-26% per arm) and the `wrong_population` bucket,
  and was the cheapest of the three build options.
  Retired because the error analysis in Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md 23.3 says it would
  attack an already-solved component. Among the 690 fine-tuned queries that executed and returned a
  COMPLETELY DISJOINT result set -- the worst cases in the run -- 80.0% already name exactly the
  gold object types and 65.8% the gold event activities. Entity selection is not where this fails.
  The 80.0% is bounded above by `allowed-entities-only` constraining the vocabulary by construction,
  but the unconstrained base arm still reaches 68.4% on the same measure, so the conclusion survives
  the confound. Also retired alongside it: constraint tuning / margin fixes, because the errors are
  disjoint rather than approximate (over-broad 5.3% + over-narrow 2.3% = 7.6% of wrong answers).

  DECOMPOSITION, DIN-SQL / MAC-SQL STYLE (2-3 weeks) -- still parked, and now BOUNDED. Break the
  question into sub-questions and build the tree incrementally, validating each node as it is added.
  Best structural fit of anything considered here, because a `BindingBoxTree` IS compositional --
  nodes and child edges -- so the decomposition matches the target formalism instead of being
  imposed on it. It also produces intermediate checkpoints to validate against, which single-shot
  generation has none of. The 2026-08-30 analysis strengthened the motivation: the measured failure
  IS compositional (notes 23.4 -- node-count agreement 31.4%, constraint-type agreement 18.0%, and
  `SAT` emitted at 14% of gold's rate).
  But it is subject to the same ceiling as the training rebalance below, because it is another way
  of fixing structure, and notes 23.7 caps all such fixes below the trivial floor. Do not build it
  until the structure ablation has measured that ceiling causally -- one experiment bounds both.

  EXECUTION-GUIDED TRAINING (4-8 weeks, currently BLOCKED). The root-cause fix: change the training
  signal from "imitate this gold query text" to "produce a query whose RESULT matches". Supervised
  imitation of gold text teaches form, which is exactly what the matrix measured -- structural F1
  roughly doubles with fine-tuning (0.24 -> 0.44) while result F1 barely moves. This is the same
  arc text-to-SQL went through, for the same reason.
  The blocker: rejection sampling needs to execute a training example, and ours cannot be executed.
  The 60,018 examples in `data/finetune_ocpq.jsonl` are synthetic with the schema inline in the
  prompt and are NOT tied to any real OCEL log -- probed the whole file for vocabulary from the five
  real training-domain logs and got container_logistics 0, hiring 0, hospital 0, renting 0 (only
  generic "lending" terms hit, 9.85%, almost certainly coincidental). So there is no log to run a
  training query against and no way to obtain a gold result set for it.
  Unblocking means inverting the generation direction: synthesise QUERIES first (programmatically,
  valid by construction), execute them against a real log, then have an LLM back-translate each to
  a natural-language question. The gold result then comes free from execution and correctness
  labels become automatic. Several text-to-SQL datasets were built this way. Risk to watch: if the
  synthesised questions are templated and easier than the test distribution, gains will not
  transfer -- the seen/unseen problem one level up (see Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md
  21.6, where only 1 of 4 families transfers its gain).

  NOT WORTH DOING: fixing the syntax-repair pass. The existing repair (src/LLM.py:1661
  `schema_fix_with_llm`) fires once, only on schema-validation failure, with a static prompt that
  does not include the actual validator error and never sees the query execute -- so it looks like
  low-hanging fruit. It is not. Assume EVERY one of the 4780 never-runnable generations is repaired
  AND that repaired queries are as semantically good as first-try successes; both assumptions are
  generous, since queries failing validation are the worse generations. Correctness then ceilings
  at 8.70% (255 -> 706 exact of 8112). It moves the denominator, not the capability -- the same
  reason `result_set_f1_executed` must never be reported alone.

- NEXT (2026-08-29) Self-consistency over execution results: sample N queries per question at
  temperature > 0, execute all, cluster by RESULT SET, return the modal result. No training and no
  new infrastructure -- `repeats` and `temperature` are already fully plumbed
  (src/eval_testset.py:1170, :1113, :1404), so generating the samples is a config change and the
  only new code is a post-hoc aggregator modelled on `_recompute_metrics`. The whole matrix ran
  greedy at temperature 0 with one sample per case, so this has never been tested.
  Two arms, each at a configuration it can actually perform in: llama31-8b-finetuned /
  allowed-entities-only / schema=on (greedy F1 0.1760, 70.4% exec) and llama31-8b-base /
  chain-of-thought-plus / schema=on (0.1023, 45.0%). Same family, each at its own best strategy, so
  fine-tuning is the only variable. Pairing base with allowed-entities-only would be vacuous -- that
  cell executed 0 of 1352 runs matrix-wide.
  Reports four numbers -- single-sample at the same temperature (the fair baseline; the greedy
  matrix number would confound temperature with voting), plain majority, majority after filtering
  empty and truncated results, and the free oracle best-of-N that upper-bounds any selector -- plus
  the diagnostic that decides what comes next: the AGREEMENT RATE. A top cluster of 8/10 means the
  model is confidently and systematically wrong and voting has nothing to work with, which is direct
  evidence for the agentic direction above; 2/10 means errors scatter and there is signal to
  exploit. Informative either way, which is why it goes first.

- DONE (2026-08-29) Every SLURM submit script was broken by the reorg and none of them had been
  run since. `019c2f2 "re-arranged the folders"` replaced the old
  `cd "${SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR:?SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR not set}"` with
  `SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"; PROJECT_DIR="$SCRIPT_DIR/.."`.
  That cannot work under sbatch: SLURM copies the batch script into its spool directory before
  executing it, so BASH_SOURCE[0] points at /var/spool/slurm/... and never at the repo. All 19
  scripts in slurm/ carried the identical two lines.
  It went unnoticed because the last cluster job predating it was the matrix on 2026-08-21, which
  ran the pre-reorg version. The first job to exercise the new construct was the self-consistency
  array 3294532 on 2026-08-29, and all 8 tasks died within seconds with
  "FATAL: /var/spool/slurm/src/check_eval_deps.py not found" -- 211 bytes of output each, byte
  identical. The check_eval_deps guard is what turned a silent wrong-directory run into an
  immediate, legible failure; without it the jobs would have failed later and more confusingly.
  Fixed with `_ocpq_resolve_project_dir`, applied to all 19 scripts: try $SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR, then
  its parent (submission from inside slurm/), then BASH_SOURCE and its parent (direct shell
  invocation), and validate each candidate against src/eval_testset.py before accepting it. An
  unresolvable case exits 1 printing both candidates it tried rather than guessing. Verified
  against all six invocation paths -- sbatch from the repo root, sbatch from inside slurm/, direct
  execution from the root, from slurm/, by absolute path from an unrelated cwd, and the orphaned
  case that must fail.
  Lesson worth keeping: a path construct that resolves correctly on a laptop can be structurally
  incapable of working under the scheduler, and a repo reorganisation is exactly when that gets
  introduced. Any submit script touched by a move should be run once on the cluster before the
  next real job depends on it.

- DONE (2026-08-29) Self-consistency smoke, job 3297883: 10 minutes, 8 tasks, all healthy.
  Re-partitioned the array from 4 to 8 case-shards per arm as a result.
  The path fix held: every task loaded its modules, resolved the project dir, imported the 9
  dependency modules and reached generation. The correct arms were dispatched -- tasks 0-3
  llama31-8b-finetuned/allowed-entities-only, tasks 4-7 llama31-8b-base/chain-of-thought-plus.
  152 runs started, 136 completed before the 10-minute cap: 87 success, 48 schema_invalid, 1
  execution_failed. No timeouts, no result_too_large, no OOM, no tracebacks.
  The question that mattered -- whether the 10 samples per case actually differ, without which
  the whole experiment is vacuous -- is answered yes, and from the logs alone. Status varies
  WITHIN a case at fixed everything-else: shard 0 case 1 gave 8 success and 2 schema_invalid
  across its 10 repeats, shard 3 case 1 flipped to invalid on repeats 5-7, shard 5 case 1
  alternated. F1 varies within a case too (shard 2 case 3: 1.0 on nine samples, 0.0 on one).
  Only sampling can produce that; temperature 0.7 is reaching generate() and the per-repeat seed
  derivation is doing its job. This was the cluster-only verification step and it is now closed.
  Also visible: real per-sample diversity in the base arm, which had produced almost nothing
  under greedy decoding -- two partial matches at F1 0.673 and 0.012 that greedy never found.
  combined_f1=None on a SUCCESSFUL execution occurs at 16.1% here against 13.0% in shard_logs_2
  and 9.7% in shard_logs. Pre-existing pipeline behaviour, same ballpark, not introduced by the
  temperature or seeding change. Not investigated further; noted so it is not mistaken later for
  a self-consistency artefact.
  WHY THE REPARTITION: measured cost ranged 12.7-96.6 s/run, varying by arm and by case far more
  than the plan assumed. Projected over a full shard that put the slowest base-arm task at
  10.8-14.1h against the 14h wall limit -- it would have been cancelled near the end, which is
  the most expensive possible failure. SHARDS_PER_ARM is now a single variable (was hardcoded in
  four places) set to 8, array 0-15, and a guard rejects a task id above 2*SHARDS_PER_ARM-1.
  Each case still runs exactly once, so the finer split duplicates nothing but the ~2.5 min
  startup: 169 cases over 8 shards is 22x7 + 15, 3380 runs total, unchanged. Worst task now
  projects ~5.4h.
  Lesson: a 10-minute smoke on the real hardware bought a per-run cost distribution that no
  amount of local reasoning would have produced, and the plan's 55s/35s estimate was wrong by up
  to 3x in the direction that costs a full day.

- DONE (2026-08-30) Self-consistency landed and the error analysis that followed it relocated the
  problem. Full write-ups in Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md 22 and 23; artifact for the
  supervisor at https://claude.ai/code/artifact/480a26cb-8e02-4de6-b641-e8f78ac17e3b
  Voting works and is free: majority vote over 10 samples raises the fine-tuned arm from 0.1705 to
  0.2080 F1 and doubles exact match on the discriminating stratum, 6.7% -> 13.3%. Agreement is a
  usable confidence signal -- 0.3542 F1 above 90% consensus against 0.0058 below 60%.
  The finding that matters is the ORACLE CEILING: best-of-10 with a perfect selector reaches only
  0.2464 against a discriminating floor of 0.5148. Every selection rule -- voting, reranking, a
  learned verifier, an execution-guided scorer, a repair loop that always repairs correctly -- is
  bounded above by that number, because none of them can return an answer that was never generated.
  The decoding-time family is closed by measurement rather than by argument.

- NEXT (2026-08-30) The structure ablation. Three conditions, 507 runs, ~6h -- the cheapest
  experiment in the backlog, and it bounds both remaining composition directions at once.
  Hand the model the GOLD tree skeleton (node count, parent/child edges, per-node variable arity)
  and, at a second rung, the gold constraint TYPES -- withholding every semantic detail: no entity
  type names, no filters, no constraint parameters. These are oracle conditions and can never be
  reported as system performance; the strategy names, the manifest flag and the recorded hint all
  say `oracle-` for that reason.
    S0  schema-allowed-entities-only            (today's prompt, run in-job for comparability)
    S1  oracle-skeleton-given
    S2  oracle-skeleton-and-constraints-given
  Decision rule, pre-registered AGAINST THE FLOOR rather than against S0:
    * S2 still below 0.5148 -> the whole composition programme is bounded below the floor. Drop
      both the rebalance and decomposition; move to grounding. Most likely and most valuable.
    * S2 clears it but S1 does not -> constraint selection is the bottleneck, not tree shape.
    * S1 clears it -> tree shape alone is the bottleneck.
    * S1 ~ S2 ~ S0 -> check hint compliance FIRST; that pattern is also what ignoring the hint
      looks like, and a null result is uninterpretable without it.
  Why it exists: notes 23.7 is observational. Conditioning on the model already getting the tree
  shape right gives F1 0.3770 / 25.7% exact, against 0.1824 / 11.6% when it does not -- shape
  doubles everything -- but 45.4% of shape-matched queries are STILL completely disjoint and 0.3770
  is below the floor, and that figure is selection-biased upward because the questions where the
  model happened to get the shape right are plausibly the easier ones. The ablation measures the
  same quantity causally.

- CONDITIONAL AND BOUNDED (2026-08-30, gated on the ablation) Rebalance the training synthesis.
  1-2 weeks including one retrain. Regenerate data/finetune_ocpq.jsonl matched to the task
  distribution rather than to whatever the generator happens to emit. Measured mismatch, over
  parsed queries in every row (notes 23.6):
                              1-box share   mean boxes   share with >=1 SAT
    training (60,018)            34.6%         2.06            22.1%
    model output                 37.3%         1.90            ~13%
    test gold (the task)          8.9%         2.51            58.6%
  The model reproduces its training distribution closely and both miss the task badly. Targets:
  mean ~2.5 boxes, ~59% of examples carrying SAT, 1-box examples cut from 34.6% to ~9%. The
  generator already exists -- this is a sampling change plus a fine-tune, not new infrastructure.
  TWO LIMITS, RECORD BOTH WITH ANY RESULT:
    1. It cannot reach the floor. Notes 23.7 caps perfectly-fixed shape at F1 0.3770 optimistically,
       against a floor of 0.5148. At best this moves 0.21 to something under 0.38 -- a reportable
       improvement, not a solution. Gate it on the ablation CLEARING THE FLOOR, not on the ablation
       merely beating S0.
    2. The synthetic data is not tied to any executable log (container_logistics 0, hiring 0,
       hospital 0, renting 0 vocabulary hits). It teaches FORM. Rebalancing form yields
       better-shaped but still ungrounded queries -- which is exactly the 45.4%-disjoint-despite-
       correct-shape row.
  Counter-evidence worth keeping in view: 15,795 training examples are already 3-box, which is not
  a starved regime, and correctness at 2 boxes (10.1%) and 3 boxes (11.2%) is near-identical with a
  cliff only at 4+ (3.3%) -- not the monotone decline a pure frequency story predicts.

- IMPLIED NEXT DIRECTION if the ablation lands below the floor: grounding, not composition. The
  model sees a type-level schema and nothing else -- never cardinalities, sample records, attribute
  values, or whether a relation it filters on occurs in the data at all. Notes 23.3 says it already
  knows WHICH types a question is about; notes 23.7 says knowing the shape too is not enough. What
  is left is knowing what is actually in the log, which is the AGENTIC WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS
  entry parked above -- and which also supplies the upper bound the paper still lacks.

- DONE (2026-08-30) Structure-ablation smoke, job 3339751: the hint format was wrong and the
  10-minute smoke caught it. Full write-up in Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md 24.
  Mechanically clean -- 15/15 invocations got a hint, zero fallbacks, no errors, 34.9 s/run, so
  ~1h45m per rung and ~5h for all three. But COMPLIANCE was 18%: the model adopted the given tree
  shape in 2 of 11 parsed predictions, with 7 collapsing to a single box after being told 2, 3 or
  4. Baseline node agreement on the same 14 questions with NO hint is 30%, so the hint was not
  helping at all. Not a delivery bug -- prompt-token deltas track the hint size case by case
  (a ~97-token hint adds 106 prompt tokens, a ~155-token one adds 185), so it was in the prompt
  and being ignored.
  Cause: the hint was PROSE ("node 0: declares 1 new event variable(s)..."), which is out of
  distribution for a model fine-tuned to emit OCPQ JSON from a schema and a question. Fixed by
  emitting gold's tree as an OCPQ JSON SCAFFOLD with <FILL> placeholders in every semantic slot --
  in distribution, and it turns "obey this instruction" into "fill these blanks".
  Also added src/structure_ablation_report.py, which reports compliance BEFORE any score and
  refuses to conclude below 70%. Below that threshold "structure does not help" and "the model
  will not take structural instruction" produce identical numbers, and only the first is a finding
  about OCPQ. It also counts result_too_large per rung and prints a matched re-score, because
  deeper trees select more and an uneven refusal count would penalise S1/S2 for a storage reason
  while biasing the result toward the conclusion the analysis already predicts.
  Lesson: the compliance gate was written into the plan as a precondition before any result
  existed, and it is the only reason this was caught -- a flat S1 would otherwise have read as
  "structure does not help", the expected conclusion reached for the wrong reason.

