- DONE (2026-09-01) Structure ablation complete, job 3369038: 3 rungs x 169 = 507 runs, all exit 0.
  Full write-up in Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md 25. THE COMPOSITION PROGRAMME IS CLOSED.
  Two findings.
  (1) STRUCTURAL GUIDANCE THROUGH THE PROMPT DOES NOT WORK. Handing the model gold's tree moves
  shape adoption from 33% of cases to 30% (S1) and 27% (S2). Paired on the same 169 questions,
  133 are unchanged, 16 flip to correct and 20 flip to wrong -- churn, not effect. Mean predicted
  tree size 1.84 / 1.65 / 1.81 against gold's 2.51. Holds across two prompt formats: prose gave
  18% compliance (notes 24), a JSON scaffold in the dialect the model was fine-tuned to emit gives
  30% against an unhinted 33%. Not a formatting accident.
  Report the PER-CASE denominator. The report's first table divides by parsed predictions and
  reads 45% -> 36% -> 36%, which looks like a real decline; it is mostly an artefact of S1 parsing
  more queries (141 vs 121, schema-invalid 48 -> 28).
  (2) CORRECT STRUCTURE IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT. Conditioning on the model producing the
  right shape: F1 0.3855 (S0), 0.3614 (S1), 0.3353 (S2) -- roughly triple the 0.06-0.13 when the
  shape is wrong, so structure is a real lever, but ALL THREE sit below the 0.5148 trivial floor.
  With notes 23.7's observational 0.3770 at n=471 that is four independent estimates converging on
  ~0.35. A system that got the tree right every time would still score below a do-nothing query.
  Also worth keeping: the hint buys SYNTAX, not semantics. S1 raises execution 71.0% -> 81.1% and
  cuts schema-invalid 48 -> 28, while exact matches go 14 -> 13. That is the notes-21 syntax/
  semantics split reproduced inside one model under a controlled manipulation rather than inferred
  across arms -- the cleanest form of that claim the thesis has.
  NOT a finding: the unconditional F1 decline (0.1799 -> 0.1422 -> 0.1246). Paired on the 135
  discriminating questions the mean difference is -0.0377 (95% CI -0.0913..+0.0160) for S1 and
  -0.0553 (-0.1111..+0.0005) for S2. Both cross zero. Direction worth a sentence, effect not
  established -- reading the summary table alone would have produced an overclaim.

- CLOSED (2026-09-01) Everything bounded below the trivial floor, with the measurement that closed
  it. Do not reopen any of these without new evidence:
    * decoding-time selection (notes 22) -- oracle best-of-10 = 0.2464 < 0.5148
    * explicit schema linking (notes 23.3) -- already ~80% correct where it matters
    * constraint tuning / margin fixes (notes 23.2) -- errors are disjoint, not approximate
    * training rebalance toward compositional depth (notes 23.6, 25.5) -- bounded at ~0.35
    * decomposition, DIN-SQL / MAC-SQL style (notes 25.2, 25.5) -- the model does not take
      structural instruction, AND correct structure would not be enough if it did
  The one experiment that would make 25.5 causal rather than selected: force the structure
  programmatically -- build the tree from gold's skeleton in code and ask the model only for slot
  contents, so adoption is 100% by construction. Roughly half a day. Not built, because four
  agreeing estimates across two runs make it confirmatory; build it if a reviewer challenges the
  sufficiency claim.

- NEXT (2026-09-01, corrected 2026-09-02) GROUNDING is what is left. This entry originally said the
  model "never sees actual attribute VALUES"; that is wrong. `build_schema_block` at level `full`
  already emits attributes with bounded string-value enumerations and the E2O/O2O graphs with
  qualifiers. What it never emits is anything counted or instance-level: type counts, activity
  frequencies, relation cardinality, numeric ranges, the log timespan, or what its own query
  returns. Notes 23.3 shows it already knows WHICH types a question concerns; notes 25.5 shows
  that knowing the shape as well is still not enough. What is left is knowing what is in the log.
  See the AGENTIC WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS entry parked above for the tool surface and the
  frontier-model caveat. It is also the only remaining direction that supplies the UPPER BOUND the
  paper still lacks -- right now there is a floor and no ceiling, which is precisely why the
  "cannot claim anything" critique lands.

- DONE (2026-09-02) RE-SCORING PASS: is part of the measured failure the instrument?
  ANSWER: partly, and it changes nothing. Full write-up in Thesis/notes/11-evaluation-readiness.md
  26. Headline, fine-tuned arm, 135 discriminating entries, per-case means: strict 0.1825 ->
  kind_projected 0.2211 against an UNCHANGED floor of 0.5148. Paired per entry: 18 improved, 0
  worsened, 117 unchanged, mean +0.0385 (95% CI +0.0149..+0.0622), so the artefact is real and
  significant -- and 0.2211 vs 0.5148 is the same verdict as 0.1825 vs 0.5148.
  THE FLOORS DO NOT MOVE TOGETHER, which is the actual result. population floor 0.5148 -> 0.8881
  (102 of 135 discriminating entries have a population floor of EXACTLY 1.0: the gold constraints
  do not change WHICH rows are selected, only which are FLAGGED -- the testset is compliance-
  shaped). violation floor is 0.0000 BY CONSTRUCTION, because a no-op baseline strips constraints
  and so flags nobody; its skill is the raw F1 in disguise and must not be quoted as skill.
  kind_projected floor is UNCHANGED, because the baseline binds gold's variables.
  So only strict vs kind_projected is a valid comparison. `--variants` prints skill_n per row and
  flags any row whose denominator differs from strict; that column caught a wrong reading of this
  same table within one paragraph of producing it.
  Knock-ons: notes 25.4 gains a mechanism (under kind_projected the S0->S1 gap almost vanishes,
  0.2292 vs 0.2247 -- the skeleton makes the model bind MORE variables, which the strict key
  punishes categorically), and notes 25.5's sufficiency bound rises from 0.3855/0.3614/0.3353 to
  0.4053/0.4211/0.4099 and STILL sits below 0.5148. Quote ~0.41 as the relaxed upper estimate
  beside ~0.35. The "necessary and not sufficient" claim survives with a narrower margin.
  Not built, and worth one line if a reviewer asks: a real floor for the violation half would be a
  FLAG-EVERYTHING query rather than the no-op one.

  (original entry, kept for the pre-registration:)
  `result_set_f1_strict` keys each root binding as (event_indices, object_indices, violated) and
  intersects those tuples, so a prediction that binds ONE EXTRA VARIABLE changes every key and
  scores exactly 0. Measured on the selfcons store, fine-tuned arm, discriminating stratum: 152
  samples (~18% of executed discriminating samples) where gold binds objects only and the
  prediction binds objects AND events have mean objects-only F1 0.5203 and mean `combined` F1
  0.0000 -- and only 4 of the 152 actually select the wrong objects.
  Implemented: `SIMILARITY_VARIANTS` in src/eval_testset.py, one projection table that both
  `_compute_similarity` and audit_testset_discriminability.py iterate, so a variant and its floor
  come from one definition. `strict` (unchanged, primary) / `population` (drop the violation flag)
  / `violation` (flagged situations only, None where gold flags nothing) / `kind_projected` (blank
  the entity kinds gold never binds; diagnostic only, never primary).
  Prediction-side per-case means, fine-tuned discriminating: strict 0.1825, population 0.2392,
  violation 0.1371, kind_projected 0.2211. A fifth variant -- making keys invariant to which
  variable slot an entity landed in -- was tested and DROPPED: it scores 0.1825, identical to
  strict, because no gold entry in that stratum binds two objects in one situation.
  THE RELAXATIONS RAISE THE FLOOR TOO, so the decision variable is skill, not raw F1. First two
  audited entries already show it: entry 1's strict floor is 0.7725 and its `population` floor is
  0.9967. Expect skill to be flat and the finding to be one honest paragraph.
  Regression gate that must keep passing: `--recompute` leaves every per-record `combined` F1 and
  every per-record `skill` bit-identical (verified 3380 records, 0 changed), and
  `summary.variants.strict` equals `result_set_f1_strict` in every block.
  Note for whoever reads the denominators: until the per-variant floors are promoted, `violation`
  undefined-counts differ between arms (128 base vs 378 fine-tuned) because a record with no
  similarity falls back to the strict 0-substitution. With floors present, undefinedness becomes a
  property of the ENTRY and the arms agree. That is the intended behaviour, not drift.

- NEXT (2026-09-02) G1: STATIC LOG PROFILE IN THE PROMPT. Days, not weeks. Extend
  `build_schema_block` (src/LLM.py) with the counted facts it omits: per-type instance counts,
  activity frequencies, relation cardinality (mean/percentile items per order, not just that the
  edge exists), numeric attribute ranges, and the log timespan. Run the same three-rung ladder as
  the structure ablation on the same 169 cases, `--model-idx 1`, greedy. Runs on the EXISTING
  fine-tuned model, so it is directly comparable to every published number -- unlike G2.
  EXPECT A SMALL EFFECT, and record why so nobody re-derives it: gold's numeric constraint
  parameters are overwhelmingly the structural idioms (min=1, max=None) and (0s, None). Of ~310
  instances across the testset, 274 are those defaults. Only ~40 of 169 cases carry a non-default
  numeric bound, and most of those numbers are stated in the question itself. The counted facts
  matter for relation cardinality and for knowing whether a filter binds anything -- not for
  picking thresholds.

- PARKED (2026-09-02) G2: INTERACTIVE GROUNDING WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS. The agentic version of
  G1 and the same direction as the AGENTIC WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS entry above; keep them
  together if either is built. Tool surface: `count(type)`, `sample(type, n)`,
  `cardinality(event, object)`, `attribute_values(type, attr)`, and the one that matters,
  `try_query(partial)` returning binding count and execution errors. Today's repair pass
  (src/LLM.py, `verify_ocpq_json` and the execution-error retry) repairs SCHEMA VALIDITY and never
  semantics: the model never learns that its query bound 4237 things where gold binds 1645.
  Three things to carry into the design:
    * PROBING IS NEARLY FREE HERE. ~34.5 s log import against ~30 ms evaluation, so a 20-probe
      budget costs under a second once the import is amortised across a session. Most agentic-query
      work pays per probe. That is a defensible design argument and belongs in the paper.
    * ORACLE DISCIPLINE MUST BE ENFORCED IN CODE, the way `oracle_conditions` was for S1/S2.
      Legitimate feedback: result size, execution errors, whether a variable binds. Illegitimate:
      anything derived from gold. And the reward must NOT be result size -- an agent optimising for
      "returns a plausible number of things" drifts toward the trivial floor query itself, which is
      the one query guaranteed to score 0.5148 while expressing nothing.
    * IT NEEDS A DIFFERENT MODEL. The fine-tuned Llama emits one JSON object and cannot hold a
      multi-turn trajectory, so G2 is a separate arm -- frontier model with tools vs fine-tuned
      small model, one shot -- and must be reported as one. That changes the comparison but supplies
      the ceiling the thesis lacks.
